---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FAQSection from "../components/FAQSection.astro";

const debtFaqs = [
  {
    question: "Does rent count as debt?",
    answer:
      "Rent is usually a housing expense, not debt. But it still affects affordability because it reduces the money left for savings, payments, and normal expenses.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does a mortgage count as debt?",
    answer:
      "Yes, a mortgage is debt. But for many affordability calculators, mortgage or rent is evaluated separately from other monthly debt payments.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do credit cards count as debt if I pay them off every month?",
    answer:
      "If you pay the card in full every month, it usually behaves more like a payment method. If you carry a balance, it should be counted as debt.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do buy-now-pay-later plans count as debt?",
    answer:
      "Yes. If you owe scheduled payments to a company, the payment reduces monthly flexibility and should usually be counted as debt.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I enter total debt or monthly debt payments?",
    answer:
      "Use the label on each calculator. If it asks for monthly debt payments, enter the monthly amount. If it asks for total debt, enter the total balance.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="What Counts as Debt? | ShouldISpend"
  description="Learn what counts as debt for affordability decisions, including credit cards, student loans, car loans, mortgages, medical debt, and monthly payment obligations."
>
  <style>
    .container {
      max-width: 920px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 80px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 0.95;
      margin-bottom: 28px;
      color: var(--text);
      max-width: 860px;
    }

    .intro {
      max-width: 800px;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.12rem;
      line-height: 1.9;
      margin-bottom: 56px;
    }

    section {
      margin-top: 64px;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
      margin-bottom: 20px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    h3 {
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      margin-top: 28px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.85;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    .callout {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      background: var(--card);
      border-radius: 24px;
      padding: 26px;
      margin: 34px 0;
    }

    .callout p {
      margin: 0;
    }

    .callout strong {
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .info-grid,
    .link-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 18px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .info-card,
    .link-card {
      display: block;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      background: var(--card);
      border-radius: 18px;
      padding: 20px;
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
    }

    .link-card {
      transition: transform 0.15s ease;
    }

    .link-card:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
    }

    .info-card h3,
    .link-card strong {
      display: block;
      margin: 0 0 8px;
      color: var(--text);
      line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .info-card p,
    .link-card span {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.6;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      margin: 0;
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">Financial Basics</div>

    <h1>What Counts as Debt?</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      For ShouldISpend calculators, debt usually means borrowed money,
      balances, or recurring payment obligations that reduce your financial
      flexibility. Debt matters because it affects whether a new rent payment,
      car payment, trip, wedding, home purchase, or major expense is actually
      safe after normal bills are paid.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Simple rule:</strong> if you owe money and must make payments
        over time, it usually counts as debt for affordability decisions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Common Examples of Debt</h2>

      <p>
        These obligations usually count as debt because they create monthly
        payments, interest costs, or repayment pressure.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Credit Card Balances</h3>
          <p>
            Count carried balances, especially when interest is being charged or
            minimum payments are required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Student Loans</h3>
          <p>
            Monthly student loan payments reduce flexibility for rent, cars,
            savings, and major spending.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Auto Loans</h3>
          <p>
            Car loans count as debt, and the payment should be viewed alongside
            insurance, fuel, maintenance, and repairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Personal Loans</h3>
          <p>
            Personal loans, consolidation loans, and installment loans usually
            count as debt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Medical Debt</h3>
          <p>
            Medical bills, payment plans, and collection balances can affect
            affordability and monthly stress.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Buy-Now-Pay-Later Plans</h3>
          <p>
            Scheduled payments from BNPL services reduce monthly flexibility and
            should usually be counted.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What Usually Does Not Count as Debt?</h2>

      <p>
        Some expenses are not technically debt, even though they still matter for
        affordability. The distinction matters because calculators often separate
        monthly bills from borrowed-money obligations.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Groceries</li>
        <li>Utilities</li>
        <li>Gas and transportation costs</li>
        <li>Insurance premiums</li>
        <li>Childcare</li>
        <li>Subscriptions</li>
        <li>Phone and internet bills</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        These costs may not be debt, but they still reduce the money available
        for saving, spending, and new payments.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What About Rent or a Mortgage?</h2>

      <p>
        Rent is usually treated as a housing expense, not debt. But rent still
        matters because it can consume a large share of take-home income and
        reduce flexibility for debt payoff, savings, transportation, and normal
        expenses.
      </p>

      <p>
        A mortgage is debt, but it is often evaluated separately from other debt
        because it is also a housing cost. When reviewing affordability, it can
        help to look at housing costs first, then other recurring debt payments.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Practical approach:</strong> separate housing from other debt,
          then judge whether the combined pressure leaves enough room for
          emergency savings and normal life.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Should You Enter the Full Balance or Monthly Payment?</h2>

      <p>
        Use the label on each calculator. Some decisions are based on monthly
        payment pressure. Others are based on total debt load.
      </p>

      <h3>Use Monthly Payments When Asked</h3>
      <p>
        Monthly debt payments show how much cash flow is already committed each
        month. This is usually the most useful number for rent, car payment,
        vacation, and wedding affordability.
      </p>

      <h3>Use Total Balances When Asked</h3>
      <p>
        Total balances help show longer-term financial pressure. A small monthly
        minimum payment can hide a large balance that may take years to repay.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How Debt Creates Financial Pressure</h2>

      <p>
  Debt changes the answer because a new purchase does not happen in
  isolation. A
  <a href="/should-i-spend-700-on-a-car-payment">
    $700 car payment
  </a>,
  <a href="/should-i-spend-5000-on-a-vacation">
    $5,000 vacation
  </a>,
  <a href="/should-i-spend-30000-on-a-wedding">
    $30,000 wedding
  </a>,
  or higher rent payment may look manageable until existing obligations are
  included.
</p>

      <p>
        The more debt you already have, the more important it becomes to protect
        emergency savings and avoid new monthly commitments.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a broader explanation, read the
        <a href="/how-much-debt-is-too-much">debt affordability guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Related ShouldISpend Guides</h2>

      <div class="link-grid">
        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-debt-is-too-much">
          <strong>How Much Debt Is Too Much?</strong>
          <span>
            Learn how debt-to-income pressure and monthly obligations affect
            major spending decisions.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-emergency-savings-should-i-have">
          <strong>Emergency Savings Guide</strong>
          <span>
            Understand how much cash cushion to keep before spending big.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-car-can-i-afford">
          <strong>How Much Car Can I Afford?</strong>
          <span>
            See how debt, insurance, savings, and income affect car
            affordability.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-this-much-on-groceries-per-month">
  <strong>Monthly Grocery Spending Calculator</strong>

  <span>
    Evaluate how groceries, dining out, debt payments, and emergency savings
    interact inside your monthly budget.
  </span>
</a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford">
          <strong>How Much House Can I Afford?</strong>
          <span>
            Evaluate home affordability using income, debt, housing costs, and
            savings.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-vacation-can-i-afford">
          <strong>How Much Vacation Can I Afford?</strong>
          <span>
            Compare travel spending against debt, savings, and post-trip
            flexibility.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-10000-on-a-funeral">
  <strong>$10,000 Funeral Calculator</strong>

  <span>
    See how unexpected major expenses interact with debt pressure,
    savings, and monthly flexibility.
  </span>
</a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/budget-rule-calculators">
          <strong>Budget Rule Calculators</strong>
          <span>
            Browse percentage-based calculators and financial decision guides.
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <FAQSection title="Debt Basics FAQ" faqs={debtFaqs} />
  </main>
</Layout>